__d(function(e,a,r,t,i,n,s){i.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:7,subject:"Mathematics",textbook:"Mathematics \u2014 Class 7 (NCERT)",chapter_number:11,chapter_title:"Perimeter and Area",estimated_study_time_minutes:55,difficulty:"Easy",learning_objectives:["Recall and apply perimeter and area formulas for squares, rectangles, and triangles","Derive and apply the area formula for parallelograms","Derive and apply the area formula for triangles (including using base and height)","Find the circumference and area of circles using \u03c0","Convert between different units of area (cm\xb2, m\xb2, km\xb2)","Solve problems involving paths, borders, and combined shapes","Apply area and perimeter concepts to real-life situations","Understand the relationship between shapes of equal area but different perimeters"],prerequisite_knowledge:["Perimeter and area of squares and rectangles from earlier classes","Properties of triangles, parallelograms, and circles","Basic operations with fractions and decimals","Understanding of \u03c0 (pi) as approximately 22/7 or 3.14"],introduction:{why_important:"Perimeter and area are fundamental measurements used in every aspect of daily life \u2014 from buying tiles for a floor, fencing a garden, painting a wall, to designing buildings. This chapter extends earlier knowledge to more shapes (parallelograms, triangles, circles) and real-life applications.",real_life_relevance:"Perimeter: how much fencing is needed for a field? Circumference: how far does a wheel travel in one rotation? Area: how many tiles cover a floor? How much paint for a wall? These are daily calculations for farmers, builders, engineers, and even home owners.",karnataka_context:"Karnataka's agricultural economy (rice fields in the Cauvery basin, sugarcane in Mandya, coffee in Coorg) constantly requires area calculations for land measurement. The traditional unit 'guntha' (approximately 101 square metres) is still used alongside modern units. Karnataka's construction boom in Bengaluru requires area calculations for apartments, commercial spaces, and IT parks. The famous Cubbon Park in Bengaluru covers 300 acres \u2014 an area calculation problem on a grand scale.",connection_to_previous_learning:"Class 6 introduced perimeter and area of squares and rectangles. Class 7 extends this to triangles (revisited with the height concept), parallelograms, and circles \u2014 and introduces more complex application problems."},core_concepts:[{concept_name:"Area of Parallelogram and Triangle",simple_explanation:"The area of a parallelogram is base \xd7 height (not base \xd7 slant side). The area of a triangle is \xbd \xd7 base \xd7 height. The 'height' must be PERPENDICULAR to the base.",detailed_explanation:"PARALLELOGRAM: A parallelogram can be converted to a rectangle by cutting a triangle from one end and placing it on the other. The resulting rectangle has the same base and height as the parallelogram. So: Area of parallelogram = base (b) \xd7 height (h), where h is the PERPENDICULAR distance between the parallel sides (not the slant side). TRIANGLE: Any triangle is exactly half a parallelogram (draw the diagonal of a parallelogram \u2014 it divides into two equal triangles). So: Area of triangle = \xbd \xd7 base \xd7 height. The height (h) must be the PERPENDICULAR distance from the base to the opposite vertex. For an obtuse triangle, the height may fall outside the triangle (but is still valid). Key: the base and height must be perpendicular to each other \u2014 you cannot use the slant side as height.",real_life_example:"A farmer has a parallelogram-shaped plot with base 20 m and perpendicular height 15 m. Area = 20 \xd7 15 = 300 m\xb2. If they build a fence along the boundary, they need the perimeter (using the actual sides, not height). A triangular park has a base of 50 m and a perpendicular height of 30 m. Area = \xbd \xd7 50 \xd7 30 = 750 m\xb2.",karnataka_example:"Karnataka's Deccan plateau has many irregularly shaped agricultural plots. Survey departments use the parallelogram and triangle area formulas to calculate plot areas from the base and height measurements. The Revenue Department of Karnataka calculates land tax based on area in square metres or guntha \u2014 directly using these formulas.",common_mistakes:"Most common mistake: using the SLANT SIDE instead of the PERPENDICULAR HEIGHT in the area formula. In a parallelogram with base 10 cm, slant side 8 cm, and height 6 cm, the area = 10 \xd7 6 = 60 cm\xb2 (NOT 10 \xd7 8 = 80 cm\xb2). Always identify the perpendicular height \u2014 it is drawn at 90\xb0 to the base.",memory_tip:"PARALLELOGRAM area: imagine pushing a rectangle into a lean \u2014 it's still base \xd7 height because you're just rearranging the same area. TRIANGLE = HALF PARALLELOGRAM. Draw a diagonal across any parallelogram \u2014 two triangles appear. Each is \xbd \xd7 base \xd7 height!"},{concept_name:"Circumference and Area of a Circle",simple_explanation:"A circle's circumference (perimeter) = 2\u03c0r or \u03c0d. Its area = \u03c0r\xb2. Here \u03c0 \u2248 22/7 \u2248 3.14, r = radius, d = diameter.",detailed_explanation:"A circle's measurements use the constant \u03c0 (pi) \u2248 22/7 \u2248 3.14159... CIRCUMFERENCE: The distance around a circle (its perimeter). C = 2\u03c0r (where r = radius) or C = \u03c0d (where d = diameter). Derivation concept: if you unroll the circumference of a circle, it stretches to approximately 3.14 times the diameter. AREA: The space enclosed inside a circle. A = \u03c0r\xb2. Derivation concept: cut a circle into many thin sectors (like pizza slices) and rearrange them \u2014 they form an approximate rectangle with length = \u03c0r (half circumference) and width = r, so area = \u03c0r \xd7 r = \u03c0r\xb2. KEY FACTS: radius = diameter/2; diameter = 2 \xd7 radius; \u03c0 is irrational (never ends, never repeats) \u2014 we use 22/7 or 3.14 as approximations. For answers: use \u03c0 = 22/7 when radius is a multiple of 7; use \u03c0 = 3.14 otherwise.",real_life_example:"A bicycle wheel has diameter 70 cm (radius 35 cm). Circumference = 2\u03c0 \xd7 35 = 2 \xd7 (22/7) \xd7 35 = 220 cm = 2.2 m. So for every one rotation of the wheel, the bicycle travels 2.2 m. To travel 1 km = 1000 m, the wheel rotates 1000/2.2 \u2248 454 times.",karnataka_example:"Karnataka's famous Mysuru Dasara chariot wheels are large circles. A traditional chariot wheel of diameter 1.4 m has circumference = \u03c0 \xd7 1.4 = 22/7 \xd7 1.4 = 4.4 m. The entire chariot procession route (about 5 km) would cause the wheel to rotate 5000/4.4 \u2248 1136 times. Bengaluru's metro circular tunnel sections use circle area calculations for construction planning.",common_mistakes:"Three common errors: (1) Confusing radius and diameter \u2014 radius = half of diameter. If diameter = 14 cm, radius = 7 cm, not 14 cm. (2) Using diameter instead of radius in area formula: area = \u03c0 \xd7 r\xb2 (NOT \u03c0 \xd7 d\xb2). (3) Forgetting to square the radius: area = \u03c0r\xb2 means \u03c0 \xd7 r \xd7 r, not \u03c0 \xd7 r \xd7 2.",memory_tip:"C = 2\u03c0r (circumference = 2 \xd7 \u03c0 \xd7 r). Area = \u03c0r\xb2 (\u03c0 \xd7 r \xd7 r). Memory trick: CIRCUMFERENCE has the word 'CIRCLE AROUND' \u2014 it goes around. AREA is the INSIDE \u2014 it squares up. 'Sir cumference' rides AROUND the circle; the AREA SQUARES up inside it!"},{concept_name:"Paths, Borders, and Unit Conversions",simple_explanation:"When a path or border is added around a shape, the outer shape has larger dimensions. The path area = area of outer shape \u2212 area of inner shape. Convert: 1 m\xb2 = 10,000 cm\xb2; 1 km\xb2 = 1,000,000 m\xb2.",detailed_explanation:"PATH PROBLEMS: If a rectangular field is surrounded by a path of uniform width w: outer length = inner length + 2w; outer width = inner width + 2w. Path area = outer area \u2212 inner area. For circular paths: outer radius = inner radius + path width. Path area = \u03c0(R\xb2 \u2212 r\xb2) where R = outer radius, r = inner radius. BORDERS: A border of width w inside or outside a shape uses the same approach. UNIT CONVERSIONS: 1 m = 100 cm, so 1 m\xb2 = 100 \xd7 100 = 10,000 cm\xb2. 1 km = 1000 m, so 1 km\xb2 = 1000 \xd7 1000 = 1,000,000 m\xb2 = 10\u2076 m\xb2. 1 hectare = 10,000 m\xb2. 1 acre \u2248 4047 m\xb2. These conversions are critical for real-world problems where dimensions are given in one unit and answers required in another.",real_life_example:"A garden 20 m \xd7 15 m is surrounded by a path 2 m wide. Outer dimensions: (20+4) \xd7 (15+4) = 24 \xd7 19 = 456 m\xb2. Inner area: 20 \xd7 15 = 300 m\xb2. Path area = 456 \u2212 300 = 156 m\xb2. If tiles cost \u20b950 per m\xb2, the path tiling cost = 156 \xd7 50 = \u20b97,800.",karnataka_example:"Bengaluru's lal Bagh botanical garden (240 acres) has winding paths that gardeners must maintain. The Nandi Hills viewpoint has a circular viewing platform with a path around it \u2014 exactly the circular path problem in this chapter. Karnataka's village tank (kere) bunds (embankments) are calculated using path-around-rectangle formulas for their maintenance budgets.",common_mistakes:"In path problems: students forget that the path adds to BOTH sides of the shape (not just one). If path width is 2 m, the outer length = inner length + 2+2 = inner + 4 (not inner + 2). Similarly for width. Also, unit conversion: 1 m\xb2 \u2260 100 cm\xb2 \u2014 it equals 10,000 cm\xb2 (100\xd7100).",memory_tip:"PATH ADDS TO BOTH SIDES: width w \u2192 outer dimension = inner + 2w (add w on each side). Think of hugging a box \u2014 you extend on BOTH sides, not just one. And unit conversion: m to cm = \xd7100, so m\xb2 to cm\xb2 = \xd7100\xb2 = \xd710,000. SQUARE the conversion factor!"}],key_terms_glossary:[{term:"Perimeter",meaning:"The total length of the boundary of a closed figure. For a circle, the perimeter is called the CIRCUMFERENCE. Units: cm, m, km. Formulas: rectangle = 2(l+b); square = 4a; triangle = a+b+c; circle = 2\u03c0r."},{term:"Area",meaning:"The amount of surface enclosed within a closed figure. Measured in square units (cm\xb2, m\xb2, km\xb2). Formulas: rectangle = l\xd7b; square = a\xb2; parallelogram = b\xd7h; triangle = \xbd\xd7b\xd7h; circle = \u03c0r\xb2."},{term:"Height (of a triangle/parallelogram)",meaning:"The PERPENDICULAR distance from the base to the opposite vertex (triangle) or opposite side (parallelogram). The height is always at 90\xb0 to the base \u2014 it is NOT the slant side. Also called the altitude."},{term:"Base",meaning:"Any side of a triangle or parallelogram that is chosen as the reference for calculating area. The height must be drawn perpendicular to the chosen base. Any side can be the base, but the corresponding height changes accordingly."},{term:"Parallelogram",meaning:"A quadrilateral with two pairs of parallel sides. Opposite sides are equal and parallel. Area = base \xd7 height (perpendicular height, NOT slant side). Perimeter = 2(a+b) where a and b are adjacent sides."},{term:"Circumference",meaning:"The perimeter (boundary length) of a circle. Formula: C = 2\u03c0r = \u03c0d, where r = radius, d = diameter, \u03c0 \u2248 22/7 \u2248 3.14. If the radius of a circle is 7 cm, circumference = 2 \xd7 22/7 \xd7 7 = 44 cm."},{term:"\u03c0 (Pi)",meaning:"A mathematical constant representing the ratio of a circle's circumference to its diameter. \u03c0 = circumference/diameter \u2248 3.14159... It is irrational (non-terminating, non-repeating). We use 22/7 or 3.14 as approximations. Use 22/7 when the radius is a multiple of 7."},{term:"Radius",meaning:"The distance from the centre of a circle to any point on its boundary. Radius = diameter/2. Denoted by 'r'. All radii of a circle are equal. The area of a circle = \u03c0r\xb2."},{term:"Diameter",meaning:"The longest chord of a circle \u2014 a straight line through the centre joining two points on the boundary. Diameter = 2 \xd7 radius. The circumference = \u03c0 \xd7 diameter = \u03c0d."},{term:"Hectare",meaning:"A unit of area equal to 10,000 m\xb2 (or 100 m \xd7 100 m). Commonly used for measuring land area. 1 km\xb2 = 100 hectares. Karnataka's agricultural land is often measured in hectares for government records and crop surveys."}],worked_examples:[{example_number:1,type:"Area of Parallelogram",question:"A parallelogram has base = 12 cm, slant side = 8 cm, and height = 6 cm. Find its area and perimeter.",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: Identify values. Base (b) = 12 cm, height (h) = 6 cm (perpendicular), slant side = 8 cm.","Step 2: Area = b \xd7 h = 12 \xd7 6 = 72 cm\xb2.","Step 3: For perimeter, use both pairs of sides. Opposite sides of a parallelogram are equal: sides are 12 cm and 8 cm.","Step 4: Perimeter = 2 \xd7 (12 + 8) = 2 \xd7 20 = 40 cm.","Answer: Area = 72 cm\xb2, Perimeter = 40 cm.","Note: The slant side (8 cm) is used for PERIMETER, not for AREA. The HEIGHT (6 cm) is used for AREA."]},{example_number:2,type:"Circumference and Area of Circle",question:"A circle has radius = 14 cm. Find its circumference and area. (Use \u03c0 = 22/7)",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: Radius r = 14 cm. Since 14 is a multiple of 7, use \u03c0 = 22/7.","Step 2: Circumference = 2\u03c0r = 2 \xd7 (22/7) \xd7 14 = 2 \xd7 22 \xd7 2 = 88 cm.","Step 3: Area = \u03c0r\xb2 = (22/7) \xd7 14 \xd7 14 = (22/7) \xd7 196 = 22 \xd7 28 = 616 cm\xb2.","Answer: Circumference = 88 cm, Area = 616 cm\xb2."]},{example_number:3,type:"Path Problem",question:"A rectangular field is 50 m long and 30 m wide. A path 5 m wide surrounds it from outside. Find the area of the path.",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: Inner dimensions: length = 50 m, width = 30 m. Inner area = 50 \xd7 30 = 1500 m\xb2.","Step 2: Path is 5 m wide on ALL sides. Outer length = 50 + 5 + 5 = 60 m. Outer width = 30 + 5 + 5 = 40 m.","Step 3: Outer area = 60 \xd7 40 = 2400 m\xb2.","Step 4: Path area = Outer area \u2212 Inner area = 2400 \u2212 1500 = 900 m\xb2.","Answer: Area of path = 900 m\xb2."]}],quick_check_questions:[{after_concept:"Area of Parallelogram and Triangle",questions:[{type:"Recall",q:"State the formula for the area of a parallelogram.",a:"Area of parallelogram = base \xd7 height = b \xd7 h, where h is the PERPENDICULAR height (not the slant side)."},{type:"Recall",q:"A triangle has base 10 cm and height 8 cm. Find its area.",a:"Area = \xbd \xd7 base \xd7 height = \xbd \xd7 10 \xd7 8 = 40 cm\xb2."},{type:"Understanding",q:"Why is the area of a triangle half the area of a parallelogram with the same base and height?",a:"Because a diagonal of a parallelogram divides it into two congruent (equal) triangles. Each triangle has area = half the parallelogram's area. So triangle area = \xbd \xd7 (parallelogram area) = \xbd \xd7 base \xd7 height."},{type:"Understanding",q:"A parallelogram has base 15 cm, slant side 10 cm, and height 7 cm. A student calculates area = 15 \xd7 10 = 150 cm\xb2. What mistake did the student make?",a:"The student used the SLANT SIDE (10 cm) instead of the PERPENDICULAR HEIGHT (7 cm). The correct area = 15 \xd7 7 = 105 cm\xb2. In area formulas, 'height' always means perpendicular distance \u2014 never the slant side."},{type:"Application",q:"A triangular garden has sides 8 m, 6 m, and 10 m. A vertical pole stands at the midpoint of the 10 m side and reaches the opposite vertex at 4.8 m height. Find the garden's area.",a:"Base = 10 m, Height = 4.8 m (perpendicular from opposite vertex to 10 m base). Area = \xbd \xd7 10 \xd7 4.8 = 24 m\xb2."}]}],key_takeaways:["Parallelogram area = base \xd7 height (perpendicular height, NOT slant side). Perimeter = 2(a+b).","Triangle area = \xbd \xd7 base \xd7 height. A triangle = half a parallelogram with same base and height.","Circumference of circle = 2\u03c0r = \u03c0d. Area of circle = \u03c0r\xb2.","\u03c0 \u2248 22/7 (use when r is a multiple of 7) or \u03c0 \u2248 3.14 (otherwise).","For path problems: outer dimension = inner dimension + 2 \xd7 path width (on both sides).","Path area = outer area \u2212 inner area.","Unit conversions: 1 m\xb2 = 10,000 cm\xb2; 1 km\xb2 = 1,000,000 m\xb2 = 100 hectares; 1 hectare = 10,000 m\xb2.","The height in area formulas is ALWAYS perpendicular to the base \u2014 never the slant side.","Same area can have different perimeters. Same perimeter can have different areas.","Karnataka connection: land measurement (guntha, hectare), Dasara chariot wheels (circumference), Bengaluru apartment areas."],mind_map:{root:"Perimeter and Area",branches:[{branch:"Parallelogram",sub:["Area = base \xd7 height (\u22a5)","Perimeter = 2(a+b)","Height \u2260 slant side"]},{branch:"Triangle",sub:["Area = \xbd \xd7 base \xd7 height","= half parallelogram","Height must be \u22a5 to base"]},{branch:"Circle",sub:["Circumference = 2\u03c0r = \u03c0d","Area = \u03c0r\xb2","\u03c0 \u2248 22/7 or 3.14","r = radius, d = diameter"]},{branch:"Path/Border",sub:["Outer dim = inner + 2w","Path area = outer \u2212 inner","Circular path: \u03c0(R\xb2\u2212r\xb2)"]},{branch:"Unit Conversions",sub:["1 m\xb2 = 10,000 cm\xb2","1 km\xb2 = 10\u2076 m\xb2","1 hectare = 10,000 m\xb2"]}]},revision_notes:"Chapter 11 extends perimeter and area to parallelograms, triangles, and circles, and introduces path problems. Parallelogram: Area = base \xd7 height (perpendicular, NOT slant side); Perimeter = 2(a+b). Triangle: Area = \xbd \xd7 base \xd7 height (perpendicular to base) \u2014 a triangle is half a parallelogram with the same base and height. Circle: Circumference = 2\u03c0r = \u03c0d; Area = \u03c0r\xb2, where \u03c0 \u2248 22/7 or 3.14. Use 22/7 when radius is a multiple of 7. Path problems: if path width = w, outer dimension = inner + 2w (path on both sides); path area = outer area \u2212 inner area. For circular paths, path area = \u03c0(R\xb2 \u2212 r\xb2) where R = outer radius, r = inner radius. Unit conversions: 1 m\xb2 = 10,000 cm\xb2; 1 km\xb2 = 1,000,000 m\xb2; 1 hectare = 10,000 m\xb2. Key mistakes to avoid: using slant side instead of perpendicular height; confusing radius and diameter; forgetting to add path width on BOTH sides; wrong unit conversion (1 m\xb2 = 10,000 cm\xb2 NOT 100 cm\xb2).",must_remember_facts:["Parallelogram area = base \xd7 PERPENDICULAR height (not slant side).","Triangle area = \xbd \xd7 base \xd7 perpendicular height.","Triangle = \xbd \xd7 parallelogram with same base and height.","Circumference = 2\u03c0r = \u03c0d.","Circle area = \u03c0r\xb2 (NOT \u03c0d\xb2).","\u03c0 = 22/7 (r is multiple of 7) or 3.14 (otherwise).","Radius = diameter \xf7 2. Diameter = 2 \xd7 radius.","Path width w \u2192 outer dim = inner dim + 2w (BOTH sides).","Path area = outer area \u2212 inner area.","1 m\xb2 = 10,000 cm\xb2. 1 hectare = 10,000 m\xb2. 1 km\xb2 = 100 hectares."]}},2595,[]);